item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated testing is unspecified."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The tested system scored 70% in one reported run.",
          "reason": "One reported score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.3 is reinterpreted as repeatability of a fixed AI system under unchanged conditions; 9.7 permits alternative precision evidence. Relevant repeatability evidence receives 0 for insufficient information, not 1 for demonstrated inadequacy. Exact-grid scoring requires no judgemental rating, so 9.6 is n/a."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly rules out repeated testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "No repeatability findings exist; this does not demonstrate unstable scores."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA 9.3, reinterpreted for AI reruns, and 9.7, repeatability remains unassessed. Its evidence rating is 0, rather than 1: confirmed absence of repeated testing is not evidence of poor consistency. Exact-grid scoring makes 9.6 n/a."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations explicitly held the stated conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The two-percentage-point range meets the report's stated stability definition."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.3 is reinterpreted for AI reruns, with the observed range evaluated under 9.7 as alternative precision evidence. It supports stability for this system and setup. The stated range criterion is not an EFPA reliability coefficient threshold and does not alone justify an excellent reliability rating."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "The developers explicitly evaluated repeated runs under unchanged stated conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "This range exceeds the report's maximum stable range of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.3 is reinterpreted for AI reruns and 9.7 assesses the range evidence. Unlike missing evidence, these results demonstrate inadequate repeatability against the stated criterion, supporting 1 for that specific precision attribute. If critical to the intended comparison, EFPA guidance restricts use to research or exceptional expert use."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation instructions do not establish that repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added instructions supply no evidence of score consistency."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.3.8 addresses software instructions separately from reliability evidence. Installation guidance supplies procedural information but does not resolve the 0 for insufficient repeatability evidence under AI-reinterpreted 9.3 and 9.7. Documentation and demonstrated measurement quality remain distinct."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A achieved the higher observed score on the evaluated puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The passage supplies no evidence establishing intelligence across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.1 and 10.1 address the defined domain and its representation; 10.5 evaluates specific score interpretations. The observed grid-performance comparison matches the narrow intended use. Broader intelligence claims lack supplied validity evidence, without thereby disproving those claims. Under 8.3.2.2, norms may be n/a for an explicitly limited raw-score ranking purpose."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "The observed scores support A's better performance on these puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Stating a broad intended use does not establish its validity."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.1, 10.1 and 10.5 require evidence supporting the claimed interpretation. The broader intended use requires broader validity evidence than the supplied grid scores provide. Validity for selecting greater general intelligence is 0 for insufficient evidence, a critical limitation invoking EFPA's research or exceptional expert-use restriction; it is not proof that A lacks greater intelligence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Test scores alone supply no demonstrated link to improved workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial supports higher correctness with A; the reported difference interval is entirely positive."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Improved correctness does not establish an improvement in an unmeasured outcome."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 10.3.1.6 addresses experimental evidence; 10.3.2 addresses relevant external criteria. Random allocation, preregistration, blinded correctness checking and reported uncertainty strengthen evidence for the stated workshop use. Percentage correctness and its interval require contextual judgement, not mechanical application of correlation thresholds. The 120 inspectors do not constitute 120 independent AI systems. Evidence does not establish safety benefits or generalization beyond the studied setting."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction prevents these probe judgements. EFPA 2025 is retained, with only relevant sections discussed as requested. Human test-taker identity, bodily capabilities, qualifications and debriefing questions are inapplicable to the AI systems as described; operator guidance and stakeholder reporting remain potentially relevant. Human sample-size anchors are not automatically transferred to AI runs. Unreported properties remain unknown rather than inadequate. No aggregate quality score is calculated. The supplied extraction contains apparent numbering and rating-text inconsistencies; none affects these judgements, and the authoritative PDF was not consulted. Instrument source: EFPA (2025), https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. This review implies no EFPA endorsement."
}
